# tests/qemu-boot.forbidden -- substrings that must NOT appear in a healthy
# boot log. One literal substring per line; blank lines and '#' comments are
# ignored. This is the COMMON set, forbidden on every boot; each root backend
# adds its own pair beside it (tests/qemu-boot.{disk,ramdisk}.{expected,forbidden}).
# These are the kernel's halt-path banners (panic.rs, exception.rs,
# irq.rs). Designed [pf] faults from stubs C/D are healthy and NOT listed.
!!! KERNEL PANIC:
# Same-EL (EL1) exception -- what a kernel dereference of a bad user pointer
# produced before slice 5.1's PT-walk copy engine. `!!! KERNEL PANIC:` catches
# it transitively (exception_entry panics), but this is the sharper canary for
# exactly the regression that slice prevents, and it can never appear in a
# healthy boot.
!!! kernel exception (vector index
!!! unexpected EL0 sync exception
!!! EL0 instruction abort
!!! EL0 data abort
do_irq: unexpected INTID
# Slice 5.5: the exec initial stack failed its EL0 check. Both spellings are
# listed because they fail independently: worker writes its own line straight to
# fd 2 (belt and braces, in case the exit-status path is what broke), and init
# prints the reaped verdict with the number of the first failing check. Either
# one appearing means a process is being handed a frame musl's crt could not
# read -- and neither can appear in a healthy boot.
minix.rs worker: stack FAIL
minix.rs init: exec stack FAIL
# Slice 5.6: hello's runtime errno check ran but disagreed. Distinct from the
# marker simply going missing (which means hello never ran at all): this spelling
# means the C program executed and got the wrong errno or return value, i.e. the
# D7 identity broke somewhere along VFS -> driver -> syscall_ret.
minix.rs hello: errno FAIL
# Slice 5.6: the two-iovec fwrite returned a short count. Distinct from the
# marker vanishing (hello never ran): this means it ran and writev mis-counted.
minix.rs hello: iov FAIL
# Slice 5.10a: the write path ran and disagreed with itself. Distinct from the
# marker simply going missing (init never got that far): this spelling means the
# write, the re-open, or the read-back executed and produced the wrong answer --
# and the failing spellings carry a byte offset, so `verify off=` at a multiple
# of 4096 is a whole lost block while `read off=28672` is the indirect arm.
minix.rs init: fs.write FAIL
# Slice 5.10b: a create/truncate probe ran and disagreed with itself. Distinct
# from the marker simply going missing (init never got that far): these
# spellings mean the probe executed and produced the wrong answer, and the ones
# carrying `off=` name the byte where it went wrong -- `fs.hole FAIL verify
# off=0` is a dropped zone pointer (the `dirty` half of the write-back
# condition), and `off=4096` is a write that disturbed the zone already there.
minix.rs init: fs.create FAIL
minix.rs init: fs.dirgrow FAIL
minix.rs init: fs.hole FAIL
minix.rs init: fs.trunc FAIL
minix.rs init: fs.leak FAIL
# Slice 5.11: a device probe ran and disagreed with itself. Distinct from the
# marker simply going missing (init never got that far): these spellings mean the
# open, read, write, or close executed and produced the wrong answer, and the
# word after FAIL names the step -- `dev.zero FAIL short` is null answering for
# zero (or a clamp), `dev.null FAIL touched` is a read that wrote, `dev.console
# FAIL write` is the table's console row not reaching TTY.
minix.rs init: dev.zero FAIL
minix.rs init: dev.null FAIL
minix.rs init: dev.console FAIL
# ...and VFS's prologue battery against the memory driver's CDEV validator.
[diag vfs] mem.deny FAIL
# ...and init's batteries against VM: a PM-only gate missing, or an unknown request
# mishandled.
minix.rs init: vm.deny FAIL
# Pre-Phase-6 chunk 2: VM refused a VM_EXEC. Both of handle_exec's failing arms
# print this prefix -- a zero image_end (a branded PT_LOAD-less image, whose
# origin must never become a heap at VA 0) and a negative rc from region::exec.
# The `[diag vm] exec nr=` expectation cannot catch these on its own, because
# expectations are first-occurrence-only: one succeeding exec satisfies it while
# every later exec fails, so without this line a boot could refuse every exec
# after the first and still pass.
[diag vm] exec FAIL
# ...and bigprog's own disagreement. Distinct from `bigprog ok: bss pages
# mapped` simply going missing (the exec never happened, or ENOEXEC'd): this
# spelling means the program ran, read its first and last .bss page, and found
# them non-zero -- the loader mapped the pages but handed over frames it did not
# zero, which is another address space's data leaking into this one rather than
# merely a fixture that did not load.
bigprog FAIL
# Pre-Phase-6 chunk 5: a fileio raw-section check ran and disagreed. Distinct
# from the marker simply going missing (fileio never ran, or never got there):
# these mean the call executed and produced the wrong answer, and the word after
# FAIL names the step, with rc= and errno= as returned -- `read FAIL open
# rc=-1 errno=22` is an open flag VFS refused (O_LARGEFILE leaking through the
# shim), `fresh-buf FAIL read ... errno=14` is the pre-fault gone.
minix.rs fileio: read FAIL
minix.rs fileio: lseek FAIL
minix.rs fileio: lseek deny FAIL
minix.rs fileio: readv FAIL
minix.rs fileio: fresh-buf FAIL
minix.rs fileio: seek-hole FAIL
# ...and the memory-and-stdio section's checks, in the same form. `exit-reset
# FAIL` is the leak's own precondition failing: the descriptor fileio leaves
# open for VFS's exit reset was not 3, so an earlier check leaked one (or the
# open itself failed), and VFS's count would be judging that leak too.
minix.rs fileio: brk FAIL
minix.rs fileio: stdio FAIL
minix.rs fileio: mmap FAIL
minix.rs fileio: malloc FAIL
minix.rs fileio: exit-reset FAIL
# Slice 6.1: TTY's SYS_IRQCTL battery disagreed with the kernel. Distinct from
# `irq.deny ok` simply going missing (TTY never got there): `irq FAIL claim` means
# the kernel refused TTY its own allow-listed line, and `irq.deny FAIL case=<i>`
# names the first battery row whose result code was wrong, with rc= as returned.
#
# `irq FAIL` is also the delivery proof going wrong: `irq FAIL bits=<w>` is a
# notification without TTY's bit in its pending word, `irq FAIL enable rc=<rc>`
# a refused IRQ_ENABLE. Either leaves the line masked and receive never turned
# on. (Until slice 6.5 it had a third spelling, for any notification after the
# proof; that guard was retired when the line became the receive line, and the
# storm line below replaced it.)
[diag tty] irq.deny FAIL
[diag tty] irq FAIL
# Slice 6.5: the console's receive side. `rx overflow`: a byte arrived with the
# 256-byte input ring full and was dropped -- the newest is the one lost, so what
# was already buffered stays in order. Printed for the first drop of a boot only.
# No checked boot feeds the console more than a ring's worth before reading it,
# so this appearing means input was lost that a test was counting on.
[diag tty] rx overflow
# `rx FAIL storm`: RX_EMPTY_STORM (64) consecutive notifications found the
# receive FIFO empty -- the line keeps firing with nothing to service. One empty
# notification is legal (the acknowledgement precedes the drain); sixty-four in
# a row is a device not dropping its line, and TTY leaves it masked. `rx FAIL
# enable rc=<rc>`: the kernel refused the IRQ_ENABLE after a drain, so the next
# byte's interrupt can never arrive. Either way the console receives nothing
# more. One substring covers both spellings.
[diag tty] rx FAIL
# Slice 6.5: init's console-read child got something other than the feeder's
# probe (tools/feed-console.sh; tests/qemu-boot.expected has the whole path).
# The child prints `minix.rs init: stdin FAIL n=<count> first=<hex|none>` --
# how many bytes it read and the first of them -- followed by `rc=-<N>` when a
# read answered an errno (rc=-11 is VFS relaying TTY's EAGAIN instead of
# parking the read), `eof` when one answered 0 (a console has no EOF), or
# `over` when one reported more than was asked for; with none of those the
# count or the bytes were simply wrong (input lost, doubled, echoed back or
# rewritten on the way). init prints two more spellings itself: `stdin FAIL
# fork rc=-<N>`, the child could not be forked, and `stdin FAIL no-verdict`,
# the child was reaped without having reported (killed before its verdict).
# Like the required `stdin ok` line this describes a FED boot: type at a hand-run
# boot and the first line entered lands in that read and prints this, which is
# correct and harmless there. Unprefixed, so every spelling is covered.
stdin FAIL

# Slice 6.2: the virtio boot probe refused a device whose register page is shared
# with another transport (spec T4 rule 1), and TTY's GETINFO denial battery
# disagreed with the kernel: `getinfo.deny FAIL case=<i> rc=<rc>` names the first
# gated selector (GET_VIRTIO_MMIO, GET_DMA) that did not answer EPERM.
[virtio] refuse
[diag tty] getinfo.deny FAIL

# virtio-blk's bring-up failed at the named step (slice 6.2, spec T12), or the
# device misbehaved while serving (`step=io`, slice 6.3: a queue fault, a failed
# re-arm; the driver answers EIO from then on). `[diag vblk] no device` is not
# forbidden HERE: both checked boots forbid it in their own files (each attaches
# the boot disk as a virtio-mmio device), but it is the healthy line of the
# hand-run MINIXRS_ROOT=nodisk boot, which this common set must still pass.
# `[diag vblk] root FAIL` is not here either: the ramdisk boot requires one
# spelling of it and the disk boot forbids them all.
[diag vblk] virtio FAIL
# Slice 6.3: vblk had a device and could not publish itself to DS, so MFS cannot
# find it and the disk boot falls back (tests/qemu-boot.disk.forbidden catches
# that too; this names the cause).
[diag vblk] ds FAIL
# Slice 6.4: the flush (spec P8). `flush FAIL status=<s>`: the device completed
# a VIRTIO_BLK_T_FLUSH with a non-zero status, and the write it followed was
# answered EIO. `flush off`: the device did not accept VIRTIO_BLK_F_FLUSH, so
# writes would be served unflushed -- printed at bring-up, root or no root. QEMU
# offers the bit on every drive this repo attaches (the per-backend files pin
# the accepted word), so on any boot with a device this means the negotiation
# lost it; a boot without a device (nodisk) prints neither.
[diag vblk] flush FAIL
[diag vblk] flush off
# Slice 6.3: a partial BDEV_WRITE disturbed the bytes it did not name
# (`FAIL clobbered`: the driver overlaid the caller's bytes on a stale bounce
# buffer instead of on the block), or the probe could not run at all.
[diag mfs] bdev.partial FAIL
# Slice 6.4: the probe of the whole-disk minor (spec P6) got something that is
# neither a protective MBR nor ENXIO. `FAIL mbr`: one whole block came back and
# it is not the start of a GPT disk -- the driver resolved minor 1 to the wrong
# extent (the root's block 0 has no boot signature; the ESP's has no 0xEE
# record). `FAIL read rc=<rc>`: any other errno, or a reply that is not exactly
# one block.
[diag mfs] bdev.minor FAIL
# Slice 6.4: the persistence probe (spec P9) could not read the boot counter or
# could not store the next one. The word after FAIL names the step, with rc= as
# returned where the step answered an errno: `open` (rc=-2: /etc/boots is not in
# the image), `read`, `value` (the read did not return exactly one decimal
# digit -- an empty file is a truncate that landed without its write), `close`,
# then the rewrite's `reopen` (the O_TRUNC open), `write`, `short` (a byte count
# other than one) and `close2`. A rewrite step failing AFTER `persist gen=<n>`
# was printed is the case this line exists for: the expected marker is already
# satisfied by then, and the next boot of the image would not count.
minix.rs init: persist FAIL
